#prob(title: [Curtis 7.7])[
    A space station is in an earth orbit with a #qty(90, "min") period. At $t = 0$, a satellite has the following position and velocity components relative to a CW frame attached to the space station: $delta vbu(r) = 1 vuu(i)$ (#unit("km")) and $delta vbu(v)_0 = 10 vuu(j)$ (#unit("m/s")). How far is the satellite from the space station #qty(15, "min") later?
] <hwk:p02>

In the Clohessy--Wiltshire (CW) frame attached to the space station, $vuu(i)$ is radial, $vuu(j)$ is along-track, and $vuu(k)$ is normal to the orbit. The frame rotates at the station's mean motion:
$
      n & = (2 pi)/T = (2 pi)/qty(5400, "s") = qty(result("02", "n"), "rad/s"), \
    n t & = (2 pi)/qty(90, "min") dot qty(15, "min") = pi/3.
$

The solution of the CW equations is:
$
    delta vbu(r) (t) & = Phi_(r r) (t) delta vbu(r)_0 + Phi_(r v) (t) delta vbu(v)_0, \
    delta vbu(v) (t) & = Phi_(v r) (t) delta vbu(r)_0 + Phi_(v v) (t) delta vbu(v)_0,
$ <eqn:cw>
with the state transition matrices:
$
    Phi_(r r) & = mat(
                    4 - 3 cos(n t), 0, 0;
                    6(sin(n t) - n t), 1, 0;
                    0, 0, cos(n t)
                ), & quad Phi_(r v) & = mat(
                                          (sin(n t))/n, (2(1 - cos(n t)))/n, 0;
                                          (2(cos(n t) - 1))/n, (4 sin(n t) - 3 n t)/n, 0;
                                          0, 0, (sin(n t))/n
                                      ), \
    Phi_(v r) & = mat(
                    3 n sin(n t), 0, 0;
                    6 n (cos(n t) - 1), 0, 0;
                    0, 0, -n sin(n t)
                ), & quad Phi_(v v) & = mat(
                                          cos(n t), 2 sin(n t), 0;
                                          -2 sin(n t), 4 cos(n t) - 3, 0;
                                          0, 0, cos(n t)
                                      ).
$ <eqn:cw-stm>

Here $delta vbu(r)_0 = vecrow(1, 0, 0) space (unit("km"))$ and $delta vbu(v)_0 = vecrow(0, 0.010, 0) space (unit("km/s"))$, so only $delta x_0$ and $delta dot(y)_0$ are nonzero. Thus, only the first column of $Phi_(r r)$ and the second column of $Phi_(r v)$ contribute to the position. At $n t = pi/3$:
$
    delta x & = (4 - 3 cos(pi/3)) delta x_0 + (2(1 - cos(pi/3)))/n delta dot(y)_0 \
    & = 5/2 (qty(1, "km")) + (qty(result("02", "Phi_rv_12"), "s")) (qty(0.010, "km/s")) \
    & = qty(result("02", "dr_x"), "km"), \
    delta y & = 6 (sin(pi/3) - pi/3) delta x_0 + (4 sin(pi/3) - pi)/n delta dot(y)_0 \
    & = (#result("02", "Phi_rr_21")) (qty(1, "km")) + (qty(result("02", "Phi_rv_22"), "s")) (qty(0.010, "km/s")) \
    & = qty(result("02", "dr_y"), "km"), \
    delta z & = 0.
$

Thus, the distance between the satellite and the space station #qty(15, "min") later is:
$
    d = abs(delta vbu(r)) = sqrt(delta x^2 + delta y^2 + delta z^2) = qty(result("02", "distance"), "km") quad qed
$

#figure(
    image("../outputs/figures/s02.png", width: 45%),
    caption: [Satellite motion relative to the space station over the #qty(15, "min") interval.],
)
<hwk:s02>

